Based on my research, I found the issue! The **random-word-api** returns an **array of words**, not a single word. Your code is trying to use the entire array as if it were a single string, which causes problems.

## The Problem

When you call:
```javascript
const response = await fetch('https://random-word-api.herokuapp.com/word?lang=it');
const work = await response.json();
```

The `work` variable is an **array** like `["parola1", "parola2"]`, not a single word. When you try to use this array directly in your input field, it only shows the first character or part of it.

## The Solution

You need to extract the actual word from the array using `work[0]`:

```javascript
async function randomString() {
  const response = await fetch('https://random-word-api.herokuapp.com/word?lang=it');
  const work = await response.json();

  // Extract the first word from the array
  const randomWord = work[0];

  console.log(randomWord);
  textInput.value = randomWord;
}

randomButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
  randomString();
});
```

## Additional Fixes for Your Code

I also noticed a bug in your original `randomButton` event listener:

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - randomUrls is a string, not an array!
const randomUrls = 'https://random-word-api.herokuapp.com/word?length=5';
const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * randomUrls.length); // This will fail!
const randomWork = randomUrls[randomIndex];

// ✅ CORRECT - Use the API directly
async function getRandomWord() {
  const response = await fetch('https://random-word-api.herokuapp.com/word?lang=it');
  const work = await response.json();
  textInput.value = work[0]; // Extract first word from array
}

randomButton.addEventListener('click', function() {
  getRandomWord();
});
```

## Summary of Issues Fixed:

| Issue | Original Code | Fixed Code |
|-------|--------------|------------|
| Response structure | `work` (array) | `work[0]` (single word) |
| Array access | `randomUrls.length` (string length) | Direct API call |
| Data extraction | Not extracting from array | `work[0]` |

The key takeaway: **Always check the response structure** when working with APIs. The random-word-api returns an array, so you need to extract the actual data using index notation!

